 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 5 pubmed records: On tree longevity. [5], Understanding tree failure-A systematic review and meta-analysis. [6], Tree mycorrhizal type and tree diversity shape the forest soil microbiota. [7], Tree diversity effects on productivity depend on mycorrhizae and life strategies in a temperate forest experiment. [8] and Seasonal patterns of nonstructural carbohydrate storage and mobilization in two tree species with distinct life-history traits. [9]. \n\nTree Longevity and Senescence\n\nTrees achieve great age not through genetically programmed meristem senescence but by resisting or avoiding extrinsic mortality agents within their realized niches [1]. \n\nTree Failure Risk Factors\n\nStem weight and tree weight also positively relate to root failure, while no relating factors were found for branch failure [2]. \n\nMycorrhizal Type and Soil Microbiota\n\nTree mycorrhizal type significantly affects fungal but not bacterial alpha diversity, and only ectomycorrhizal tree species pairs show increased fungal alpha diversity with greater tree diversity [3]. \n\nMycorrhizal Type and Productivity\n\nTree species richness positively relates to community productivity, strengthening over time, but arbuscular and ectomycorrhizal trees influence productivity differently with no overyielding when combined [4]. Ectomycorrhizal communities show low initial productivity but increasing increment and strong biodiversity effects, while arbuscular mycorrhizal communities show the opposite pattern [4]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: On tree longevity [5]. Pubmed title: Understanding tree failure-A systematic review and meta-analysis [6]. Pubmed title: Tree mycorrhizal type and tree diversity shape the forest soil microbiota [7]. Pubmed title: Tree diversity effects on productivity depend on mycorrhizae and life strategies in a temperate forest experiment [8]. Pubmed title: Seasonal patterns of nonstructural carbohydrate storage and mobilization in two tree species with distinct life-history traits [9]. "
